{
  "rules": [
    "When the model forecasts a steady upward trend (e.g., +0.03/day) while recent history shows high volatility or a downward channel, apply a mean reversion correction by reducing the forecasted price to align closer to the historical average of the last 18 days.",
    "If sentiment is near zero (neutral/low) but the model predicts a significant spike based on momentum, reduce the forecast magnitude by approximately 15-20% as true outcomes often fail to sustain linear growth during low-sentiment periods.",
    "When the model forecasts a continuous rise after a recent price drop, check for 'overshoot' bias; if the last 3 days of history are declining while the model rises, invert the forecast direction or cap it at the previous day's high minus a volatility buffer.",
    "In low-sentiment environments (sentiment < 0.2), the model tends to overestimate the speed of recovery; apply a lag correction by shifting the forecast curve rightward by 5-7 days relative to the historical trend.",
    "If the true outcome shows a sharp spike followed by an immediate reversal (volatility > 10%), reduce the model's projected peak value by the amount of the subsequent drop to account for mean-reverting behavior common in EU ETS during neutral sentiment."
  ]
}
